# Time is an external dependency

`Date.now()`, `new Date()` for the current moment, `setTimeout`, `setInterval`, and `process.hrtime` are environment APIs. Application code does not control what they return or when they fire. A function with the same arguments can disagree with itself a millisecond later.

Decisions that need one moment receive a timestamp. Read the clock once at the boundary and pass that value through the operation so every rule shares the same `now`. Do not inject a clock into a comparison of two numbers.

`new Date(explicitTimestamp)` is deterministic. Parsing dates belongs at a validation boundary. Formatting belongs near the UI.

```typescript
type Session = { readonly expiresAtTimestampInMilliseconds: number };

// ❌ Hidden clock. The signature claims the session is the only input.
function isSessionExpired(session: Session): boolean {
    return Date.now() >= session.expiresAtTimestampInMilliseconds;
}

// ✅ Complete decision. Mocha asserts a comparison, not the machine clock.
function isSessionExpired(options: {
    readonly currentTimestampInMilliseconds: number;
    readonly session: Session;
}): boolean {
    return options.currentTimestampInMilliseconds >= options.session.expiresAtTimestampInMilliseconds;
}
```

Orchestration that observes time repeatedly or schedules work injects `WallClock`. See inject-wall-clock.mdc.
